    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "High-grade gliomas (HGG) are the leading cause of brain tumor death. Despite advancements in therapeutics, prognoses for patients remain poor with current treatment methods. Recently, it has been shown that neuronal activity promotes HGG growth via the activity-dependent cleavage and secretion of neuroligin-3 (Nlgn3), an important synaptic molecule and newly recognized glioma mitogen. However, the mechanisms of its cleavage and secretion are largely uncharacterized. Understanding both the cell-of-origin of Nlgn3 and the mechanism of enzymatic cleavage would shed light on the molecular interactions occurring in the glioma microenvironment, which in turn could inform therapeutics. Using a genetic knockout mouse model, this study demonstrates that Nlgn3 is necessary for glioma growth in vivo. Analyses of conditioned media generated from acute cortical slices in the presence of specific protease inhibitors showed that Nlgn3 is secreted from neuron and oligodendrocyte precursor cell populations, and cleaved enzymatically by the ADAM10 sheddase. Furthermore, administration of an ADAM10 inhibitor robustly blocks the growth of xenografted HGG cells in vivo. The results of this study highlight the therapeutic potential of targeting Nlgn3 cleavage and secretion in the glioma microenvironment and offer a novel and promising way to limit the proliferation of this group of deadly brain tumors.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
